  note    = {The one paper found whose stated purpose is to generalize required target occupancy across target classes: "In the case of antagonists, approximately 60-90\% target occupancy is required for G protein-coupled receptors, neurotransmitter transporters, and ligand-gated ion channels. Effective doses of agonists occupy a wider range of their target sites, dependent upon the intrinsic activity of the agonist, the receptor or ion channel reserve of the target site, and the response that is measured, with low efficacy agonists generally requiring high degrees of occupancy while high efficacy agonists generally require low degrees of occupancy" (abstract, PubMed MCP get_article_metadata, read 12 October 2026). **Only the abstract was read**: the article is closed, has no PubMed Central record, and the publisher's article page refused an automated reader (no robots file served) on 13 October 2026. Whether it contains per-drug occupancy tables is unknown; licence unverified. Section 2 and item 1 of "What could not be verified" in docs/pet_occupancy_feasibility.md}
}

